<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matsu Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Matsu Islands are an archipelago off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> in mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, governed by the Republic of China (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>) as Lienchiang County. Held by Nationalist forces after 1949, they were heavily fortified and shelled during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a> crises. Military rule there lasted until 1992, and the islands now draw visitors to their military sites and stone villages.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matsu-islands.jpg" alt="Matsu Islands" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sleepingstar · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matsu_Montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Matsu Islands lie in the East China Sea alongside the southeastern coast of mainland China. They form Lienchiang County, the smallest county in the Taiwan Area by area and population. The five major islands are Nangan, Dongju, Xiju, Beigan and Dongyin; Dongyin is the northernmost and Dongju the southernmost. Dongyin is slightly over 10 nautical miles (19 kilometres) from the mainland. The islands are mainly granite and diabase, the soil is not ideal for farming, and the highest point, on Beigan, is 298 metres. The climate is subtropical maritime, foggy in spring and summer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Chinese from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fukien</a> and Chekiang began migrating to the islands during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a>; most of the people of Matsu came from Houguan, today Changle in Fujian. Net fishing laid the base for the settlement at Fuao. Some crewmen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He</a> stayed on the islands for a time during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> gathered there and the residents left temporarily. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penghu">Penghu</a>, the islands were not ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> in 1895. In 1912 the British established the Dongyong Lighthouse on Dongyin Island.</p><p>The historical Lienchiang County lay partly on the mainland. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> it was effectively divided in 1949: the mainland portion came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>, while the offshore islands stayed under the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, so that the county is the only former geographical unit of its name now divided between two administrations. Large numbers of soldiers were stationed there after 1949 and during the First and Second Taiwan Strait Crisis in 1954 and 1958 respectively, and the population reached a peak of 17,088 in 1971. It then fell as young people left for lack of work. County magistrates were appointed until 1993.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The islands are administered as Lienchiang County with Nangan Township as the county seat; it has no city or urban township. The native speech is the Matsu dialect, a subdialect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuzhou">Fuzhou</a> dialect. The islands are named after the sea goddess Mazu, and the Matsu Nangan Tianhou Temple holds the sarcophagus of Lin Moniang. Tourism, military sites such as the granite tunnels, and the Matsu Distillery are central to the economy. Since 1990 a bird sanctuary has protected the Chinese crested tern, found nesting there in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Matsu Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of China today governs Taiwan, Penghu, Kinmen and <b>Matsu</b>, with about 23 million people, and is formally recognised by only a small number of states, while the People’s Republic claims the island and has not renounced the use of force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
